  "published": "2026-11-25T19:34:30.7Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.2.0:\n  What's changed\n\n  - artx signs in by itself, like txc: artx config auth login (browser, or a device code in containers and over SSH), --identity for managed identities including the agentbox sidecar, and service principals with a secret, certificate or federated credential.\n  - Artifact commands now take SharePoint and OneDrive URLs, through Microsoft Graph by default (ARTX_DRIVE_API=sharepoint switches to SharePoint's own API).\n  - Credentials and secrets are stored safely across concurrent processes, separately per configuration folder.\n\n  Impact\n\n  - Minor release. Editing files in SharePoint or OneDrive needs an app registration with file permissions: artx config auth login --client-id <id>. With the built-in default client SharePoint refuses to serve file contents; a shared TALXIS app will follow.\n\n0.1.0:\n  What's changed\n\n  - First release of artx (dotnet tool install -g TALXIS.CLI.Artifacts): one set of commands to read, patch, validate, create and convert documents and diagrams.\n  - Word: read text, structure, revisions and comment threads; 20 patch operations, including tracked changes, tables, images, content controls and comment threads with opt-in @mentions (--allow-mentions).\n  - draw.io: read, patch, validate and create diagrams, and render them to SVG.\n  - Local files are written with compare-and-swap and a lock, keep a version history (artifact history, artifact restore), and --rebase re-applies a plan on top of concurrent changes.\n  - Added artifact preview, a local live preview of a file while it is being edited.\n  - Hostile files are rejected with content-too-large instead of exhausting memory, and get marks document content as untrusted and lists hidden text.\n\n  Impact\n\n  - Initial release (0.x): commands and JSON output may still change before 1.0. SharePoint/OneDrive and Graph PDF rendering are not available in the CLI yet.",
